description:
Taught in Italian.
Required of majors and minors in the Italian Studies track.

A content-based and writing-focused course. As its thematic content changes every semester, the course may be repeated multiple times.

By taking Italian 101, students will 
•	strengthen and refine their writing skills; 
•	expand their vocabulary; 
•	enrich their fluency and familiarity with more complex language structures;  
•	acquire intercultural competence; 
•	use their critical thinking and problem solving skills to imagine creative solution to crucial issues; 
•	collaborate with peers in refining their writing process via discussions, reviews and teamwork activities.

You will read, analyse and discuss texts from a variety of different genres and writing styles (newspaper and magazine articles, essays, personal narratives, etc.). Language structures will be reviewed in the context of the texts and genres analyzed.

You will experiment and create different types of texts of varied length and purpose: letters, film reviews, opinion pieces, argumentative writing, etc., that you’ll then revise through a process or rewriting.  You will review your peers’ work while taking advantage of your peers’ feedback on your own work. 
At the end of the semester, you will have created an original portfolio of about 20-30 pages completely in Italian.

The course is entirely and exclusively taught in Italian.

